 # Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF)
+
 ## Overview
-Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) [S1] is a radiance field representation that builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting [KKLD23] by replacing standard 2D projection rasterization with ray-tracing-based volume rendering [S1]. Unlike 3DGS, which projects 3D Gaussians onto the image plane as 2D Gaussians, GOF analytically evaluates the maximum Gaussian contribution along each pixel ray [S1]. This approach provides accurate depth estimation and facilitates the derivation of level-set surfaces from accumulated opacity [S2, unofficial].
+Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) is a radiance field representation that builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by replacing the standard 2D projection rasterization with ray-tracing-based volume rendering [S1]. Unlike 3DGS, which projects 3D Gaussians onto the image plane as 2D ellipses, GOF analytically evaluates Gaussian contributions along pixel rays to compute exact depth and opacity, enabling more accurate geometric regularization and surface extraction [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of $K$ 3D Gaussians $\{G_k\}$, each defined by center $\mathbf{p}_k \in \mathbb{R}^3$, scaling matrix $\mathbf{S}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$, and rotation $\mathbf{R}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$ [S1]. Camera origin $\mathbf{o}$ and direction $\mathbf{r}$ (or $\mathbf{d}$) [S1].
-*   **Output:** Pixel color $c(\mathbf{o}, \mathbf{r})$ and depth information derived from ray-Gaussian intersections [S1].
